"""Render the audited aggregate; no distance search."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
read=lambda name:json.loads((HERE/name).read_text())
effects=read('effects.json')
accounting=read('accounting.json')
decision=read('decision.json')
lines=['# Full-block kernels and Stern collision search','',
'The full single-block kernels are the useful candidate in this experiment. The fixed four-row Stern join does not improve the final block bounds over ordinary pair scoring on these inputs. This is a structural improvement on three affine codes, not evidence of general thousand-qubit scaling.','',
'The frozen plan is [PLAN.md](../../strategy_prototypes/block_collision/PLAN.md). All five methods receive identical matrix-derived logical initialization. The four factorial methods share one native C++ engine; only the supplied space and collision switch differ. Targets, submitted witnesses and case names do not enter the search. Two equal coordinate blocks are supplied as construction metadata, so arbitrary relabeling recovery is not established here.','',
'## Controlled results','',
'Every entry lists the three seed outcomes. Lower is better; values are witnessed upper bounds. Budgets are per code on one CPU, divided equally between X and Z, with cold setup and export costs included.','',
'| Input label | Method | 2 seconds | 10 seconds |','|---|---|---|---|']
for c in effects:
    if c['grid']!='screen-2s':continue
    other=next(x for x in effects if x['grid']=='confirmation-10s' and x['case']==c['case'] and x['method']==c['method'])
    lines.append(f"| {c['case']} | {c['method']} | {c['weights']} | {other['weights']} |")
lines += ['',
'The reference bounds before this run were 90, 70 and 81, respectively. The first was already obtained cheaply by the preceding orbit restriction. The new sibling witnesses lower 70 to 51 and 81 to 54. Attribution matters: common initialization alone gives 63 on the third input, so the block-search gain over the refreshed guided control is 63 to 54. On the second input it is 72 to 51. The filename labels retain the original submission names; they are not current distance claims.','',
'In the short grid, block-pairs reaches weight 51 in 13–16 ms and weight 54 in 21–23 ms, both in the first (X) sector and including preparation. Weight 90 appears about 22–24 ms into Z, after the scheduled one-second X allocation. These are observed first-hit timings on three seeds, not guaranteed runtime or separate short-budget trials.','',
'Both block methods reproduce the short-grid final bounds across all three seeds. The collision engine does find improving four-row candidates, including some final witnesses, but ordinary pairs reach the same final block weights. Full-code collision matching has mixed outcomes against full-code pairs and stays behind the block methods. This tests a fixed p=4 Stern-style join, not Dumer partial elimination, BJMM, or a tuned state-of-the-art ISD implementation. External tools were not rerun in this study.','',
'## Why the block space helps','',
'The original full kernels have dimensions 346–348. Restricting support to one coordinate half leaves dimensions 20–40 while retaining nonzero logical images. The earlier orbit ansatz imposed an additional symmetry and missed useful sibling witnesses. Removing that constraint exposes them without searching the full kernel.','',
'The short grid records 1,727,235 block-pair reductions versus 16,843 full-pair reductions over the same aggregate allocated time. These are different-sized reductions, not equivalent trials. Accounted persistent vector buffers are 7.5–9.9 KiB per block-pair session, 8.0–11.9 KiB for block-Stern, 79.1–79.5 KiB for full-pairs and 227.3–229.1 KiB for full-Stern. The VM reports 32 KiB L1 data cache and 1 MiB L2 per core. Buffer counts exclude Python state, object/RNG overhead and improvement exports; they are not total process memory or measured cache-miss rates.','',
'## Exact checks and cluster relevance','',
'The separate fixed-cap enumeration only runs full-block kernels of dimension at most 20. Its results are below; larger kernels are explicitly skipped. Exact traversal of one block does not certify the full code.','']
exact=json.loads((HERE/'exact-blocks/audit-block-collision.json').read_text())['sessions']
for s in exact:
    lines.append(f"- {s['case']} {s['side']} {s['label']}: dimension {s['dimension']}, visited {s['enumerated']:,} nonzero combinations, minimum nontrivial weight {s['best']}, complete={s['enumeration_complete']}.")
lines += ['',
'Post-hoc matrix diagnostics reveal a stronger follow-up than generic cluster growth: the blocks producing sibling weights 51 and 54 each split into two disconnected 171-qubit components, each with kernel dimension 19. The existing winning exports lie within individual components. Enumerating each component would replace a 2^38 whole-block traversal with two 2^19 traversals. For a disconnected check graph, a nontrivial kernel word must have at least one nontrivial component, so the minimum over components is also the minimum in that decomposed block. Component enumeration has not been run in this experiment.','',
'Keep cluster search second tier. The block restriction lowers maximum check weight from 12 to 6 on the first input and from 8 to 4 on the siblings, but components still contain 171 or 342 qubits. The [dist-m4ri connected-cluster method](https://github.com/QEC-pages/dist-m4ri/blob/master/introduction.md) grows support at unsatisfied checks; lower check degree helps its branching, but does not establish feasibility at weights 50–90. A bounded sparse residual or a component too large for kernel enumeration would be a more focused trial. Variable elimination can densify checks, so a smaller kernel alone is not a reason to expect cluster search to work.','',
'## Decision and evidence','',
'Continue with block/component decomposition: the predeclared continuation criterion is met by new lower witnessed bounds on two inputs. Deprioritize the present collision configuration. The next bounded experiment should enumerate the dimension-19 components, then test whether the same matrix-derived decomposition exposes low-weight logicals in additional large codes. Preserve a generic fallback because a minimum spanning both coordinate halves can be lower. No broad parameter sweep or additional budget escalation was performed.','',
f"The three grids contain {sum(a['configurations'] for a in accounting)} configurations and {sum(a['allocated_search_seconds'] for a in accounting):g} allocated search seconds (540 randomized, 12 exact-check caps). All {sum(a['saved_documents'] for a in accounting):,} saved witness documents passed the evidence audits. Eighteen synthetic unit tests passed, including an independent four-row collision oracle, original-basis membership, chunked exhaustive traversal and save-failure propagation. Trusted verifier integrity passed for all 27 pinned files. Witnesses were checked for commutation and non-membership in the stabilizer rowspace using trusted algebra and saved through the research kit. The full submission-quality gate was not run; these are upper-bound witnesses, not new-code acceptance or exact-distance certificates.",'',
'Raw exports, deadline accounting, source archives and binary hashes are retained in [screen-2s](screen-2s/REPORT.md), [confirmation-10s](confirmation-10s/REPORT.md) and [exact-blocks](exact-blocks/REPORT.md). [effects.json](effects.json) separates session-level collision improvements from whole-sector incumbent improvements. [accounting.json](accounting.json) reports measured search and validation time separately. [decision.json](decision.json) evaluates the frozen continuation criterion. [witness-index.json](witness-index.json) links six portable copies of the best already kit-saved sector witnesses. Matrix and component diagnostics are read-only. [Reproduction instructions](REPRODUCE.md), package/hardware records, test logs and SHA256SUMS accompany the report. Previous measured sources and binaries were preserved; no verifier or leaderboard files were changed.','']
